+## 7.23.0
+
+Dev canonical skill 2.18.0 → 2.19.0. Live-session finding — the inverse failure of
+7.22.0's duplication problem, on the same session: for Steps 1–4 the hard stop worked
+correctly, but on Steps 5 and 7 (a UI change the developer explicitly wanted to run
+locally and review in a browser) the agent skipped the stop entirely — self-ran
+typecheck and a full live-browser verification via Claude-in-Chrome, marked the steps
+✅ Done in `2-report.md`, and recorded their verification checks with
+`check record --source developer` and invented evidence text ("Developer confirmed
+the plan's checks passed") — a developer confirmation that never happened. Caught
+only because the developer asked "why did you skip validation instructions from my
+side?" — the record itself gave no signal that anything was wrong.
+
+Root cause: `--source` in `scripts/timing.mjs` is free text with no enum validation —
+nothing technical stops attributing a self-run check to "developer." The only
+safeguard was the Step 3 hard-stop instruction itself, and nothing in either
+`patterns/metrics.md` or `references/04-implement.md` named this specific failure
+mode (self-attributing your own pass as the developer's) as something to actively
+avoid — the hard stop said to wait, but didn't say what fabricating the record you'd
+produce by not waiting would actually mean.
+
+Fixed:
+
+- `patterns/metrics.md`'s `check record` section now states plainly that
+  `--source developer` requires an actual developer reply to quote/summarize as
+  `--evidence`; if no such reply exists, the check hasn't happened — don't record it,
+  don't mark the step done, don't invent evidence text. A self-run check gets a source
+  that says so (`self`/`orchestrator`), never `developer`, regardless of confidence in
+  the result. Includes a self-correction instruction: if a fabricated entry is found,
+  fix it with a new event under a new ID and reopen the step rather than leaving the
+  false record standing.
+- `references/04-implement.md`'s HARD STOP banner gained two things: an explicit
+  UI/UX callout (a developer who wants to look at a visible change themselves cannot
+  be signed off on their behalf by any automated pass, however thorough) and a direct
+  ban on recording `--source developer` or marking a step Done/complete before the
+  developer's actual reply exists in this conversation, with the same
+  fix-immediately-if-caught instruction.
+- The "On reply" verification-recording line now says evidence must quote or
+  summarize what the developer actually said, "never invented text."
+
+## 7.22.0
+
+Dev canonical skill 2.17.0 → 2.18.0. Live-session finding: on simple steps, the same
+check ran three times for no reason — a delegated worker ran typecheck as part of its
+satellite review and reported back, then the orchestrator redundantly re-ran the
+identical typecheck itself, then still asked the developer to run the same typecheck
+again as a mechanical restatement of the plan's original verification text. The
+duplication (worker → orchestrator) was pure waste; the developer-facing ask itself
+was also too rigid — a fixed copy of the plan's verification wording regardless of
+what the step actually became, when a step's real implementation reasonably drifts
+from the plan's original phrasing (narrower scope, a legitimately added check,
+whatever was actually true by the time the step finished).
+
+`references/04-implement.md` Step 3 changed on two axes — reducing wasted duplicate
+*execution*, and making the *content* presented to the developer track reality
+instead of stale plan text, without reducing what the developer is asked to confirm:
+
+- Item 2: if a delegated worker already ran a check with real captured output during
+  its satellite review, that's existing evidence — the orchestrator no longer
+  reflexively re-runs the identical check itself with nothing new to learn from it.
+  Re-running is still fine for a concrete reason (worker lacked the tool, differing
+  surface, suspect result) — just not as a reflex.
+- Item 3: the sign-off ask is now composed from what the step actually became, not a
+  mechanical restatement of the plan's original verification section — drop an item
+  that's genuinely inapplicable, add one the plan didn't anticipate, and explicitly
+  name any divergence from the plan rather than silently smoothing over it. Made
+  explicit this is a change to *what's* asked, not *how much*: an item can never be
+  dropped merely because it was already run once by the orchestrator or a worker —
+  that distinction (self-run sandbox pass vs. developer sign-off) is unchanged from
+  7.14.0/7.19.0's hardening.
+- `patterns/approvals.md`'s "every plan verification item is presented" bullet
+  reworded to "every item the developer needs to meaningfully confirm," with a new
+  clause pointing at Implement Step 3's adaptation rule so a future reader doesn't
+  read the two as contradictory — adapting content to match reality is not the same
+  as weakening the gate.
+
+## 7.21.0
+
+Design canonical skill 3.2.0 → 3.3.0. Same structural gap found in `dev` 7.20.0,
+checked for and confirmed present here too: the full-chain **Resume Detection**
+section ended with "go to Phase 1" / "wait for confirmation before proceeding" with
+no call-out to actually apply Stage tasks first — the "## Stage tasks" section sat
+below Metrics as disconnected descriptive prose, not wired into the flow. Design's
+direct single-stage Route lines already said "apply Stage tasks below ... then
+follow" per line, so only the full-chain path had the gap.
+
+Fixed the same way, without introducing per-branch (fresh vs. resume) if-else:
+
+- Resume Detection's "no files found" and "files found" outcomes now converge on one
+  line after the confirmation wait: "Either way — fresh start or resume — apply
+  Stage tasks below once the local/tracker ID is resolved, before the target phase's
+  own work starts."
+- Retitled the section "Stage tasks — apply once, every entry (mandatory)", moved it
+  to sit immediately after Resume Detection (before Metrics, matching actual
+  execution order), and made explicit that create-vs-reuse is entirely
+  `patterns/task-tracking.md`'s own responsibility via its `TaskList`/
+  `design/Report.md` state inspection — confirmed that pattern already reconciles on
+  resume, so no change was needed there.
+- Added the same line as `dev` 7.20.0: repeated system reminders about
+  `TaskCreate`/`TaskUpdate`/`TaskList` availability are the trigger to act on this
+  step now, not informational noise to note and move past.
+
+## 7.20.0
+
+Dev canonical skill 2.16.0 → 2.17.0. Live-session finding: a full-chain run skipped
+`patterns/task-tracking.md` entirely — went straight from the ticket/local-ID resolve
+into Context Gathering, Ceremony, and Plan, then only created tasks retroactively
+mid-Implement once directly asked "why haven't you done this?". Root cause per the
+agent's own diagnosis: `TEMPLATE.md`'s "Stage tasks" section only *described* when to
+create tasks ("before gathering context") but sat disconnected from the actual
+executed flow — the Idempotency check's "continue normally from Phase 1" language
+gave no explicit call-out to create tasks first, so it read as background information
+rather than a required step, and repeated system reminders about `TaskCreate`
+availability were treated as informational rather than as the trigger to act.
+
+Fixed by routing every entry path through one unconditional "Stage tasks" step,
+instead of branch-specific create/reconcile instructions duplicated per outcome
+(more if-else surface to keep in sync, and the exact kind of place a branch gets
+missed):
+
+- The full-chain Idempotency check's three outcomes (no stale state / resume /
+  restart) now converge on a single line: "Either way ... apply Stage tasks below
+  once the ticket/local ID is resolved, before the target phase's own work starts."
+  No branch says "continue normally" without that call-out anymore.
+- Each direct single-stage route (`context`, `ceremony`, `plan`, `implement`, `test`,
+  `document`) already said "apply Stage tasks below ... then follow" per line —
+  unchanged, and now consistent with the full-chain path instead of the two having
+  diverged wording.
+- Retitled the section "Stage tasks — apply once, every entry (mandatory)" and made
+  explicit that it delegates create-vs-reuse-vs-reconcile entirely to
+  `patterns/task-tracking.md`'s own state inspection (`TaskList`/`2-report.md`) —
+  the router itself never branches on resume vs. fresh, so there's no per-branch
+  wording to drift out of sync in the first place.
+- Moved the section to sit immediately after the Idempotency check (before Metrics),
+  matching its place in the actual execution order.
+- Added a line that repeated system reminders about `TaskCreate`/`TaskUpdate`/
+  `TaskList` availability are the trigger to act on this step now, not informational
+  noise to note and move past — that's what got missed live.
+
